# Landing Page Development Rules

## General Principles

- Preserve the existing visual design, layout, content, and user experience unless explicitly asked to change them.
- Prefer simple, maintainable solutions over unnecessary abstractions.
- Inspect the existing codebase before making changes.
- Keep changes focused and avoid modifying unrelated files.
- Do not introduce new dependencies unless they are clearly necessary.
- Follow the existing project structure, naming conventions, and formatting style.

## HTML and Semantics

- Use semantic HTML elements such as `header`, `nav`, `main`, `section`, `article`, and `footer`.
- Maintain a logical heading hierarchy. Use one primary `h1` per page.
- Use accessible labels for form fields, buttons, and interactive controls.
- Use meaningful `alt` text for informative images.
- Use empty `alt=""` for decorative images.
- Prefer native HTML elements and browser functionality before adding JavaScript.

## CSS and Responsive Design

- Use the existing styling approach and design tokens where available.
- Build mobile-first responsive layouts.
- Avoid fixed widths that cause horizontal scrolling.
- Use reusable classes and variables instead of duplicating styles.
- Preserve spacing, typography, colors, and responsive behavior when refactoring.
- Test layouts at mobile, tablet, and desktop widths.
- Ensure visible focus states for keyboard users.
- Do not use inline styles unless there is a strong reason.

## JavaScript

- Keep JavaScript minimal and progressive-enhancement focused.
- Use clear, descriptive variable and function names.
- Avoid global variables.
- Handle missing DOM elements safely.
- Add error handling for forms, API requests, and other asynchronous operations.
- Do not store secrets or private credentials in frontend code.
- Clean up event listeners and timers where appropriate.

## Performance

- Optimize images and use modern formats when possible.
- Avoid unnecessary JavaScript and large dependencies.
- Use lazy loading for below-the-fold images where appropriate.
- Do not block page rendering unnecessarily.
- Preserve or improve Core Web Vitals.
- Avoid loading third-party scripts unless they are required.

## Accessibility

- Follow WCAG-oriented best practices.
- Ensure sufficient color contrast.
- Make all interactive functionality usable with a keyboard.
- Do not rely on color alone to communicate information.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences where animations are used.
- Ensure buttons and links have clear, descriptive text.

## SEO

- Preserve or improve page titles and meta descriptions.
- Use semantic headings and descriptive link text.
- Keep canonical URLs and social sharing metadata consistent.
- Do not remove structured data without checking its purpose.
- Ensure important content is available in the initial HTML.

## Testing and Validation

Before completing a change:

- Check for console errors.
- Test the affected functionality on desktop and mobile layouts.
- Verify keyboard navigation for interactive elements.
- Check links, forms, images, and responsive behavior.
- Run the project’s available linting and formatting commands.
- Confirm that no unrelated behavior or visual styling was changed.
